frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:70:  { text: `In eighteen sixteen, the eruption of Mount Tambora in Indonesia threw so much ash into the atmosphere that the following year had no summer. Crops failed across Europe and North America. Famine spread. Horses starved and were eaten. A young woman named Mary Shelley, trapped indoors by endless cold rain at a villa on Lake Geneva, wrote a story to pass the time. That story was Frankenstein. One of the greatest novels in the English language exists because a volcano on the other side of the world erased an entire season.`, type: 'History', styles: ['cinematic', 'gothic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:166:  { text: `Cleopatra lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the construction of the Great Pyramid of Giza. The pyramid was completed around twenty-five sixty BC. Cleopatra ruled from fifty-one to thirty BC. Apollo eleven landed in nineteen sixty-nine AD. The gap between the pyramid and Cleopatra is roughly twenty-five hundred years. The gap between Cleopatra and the Moon landing is about two thousand years. When Cleopatra looked at the pyramids, they were already more ancient to her than she is to us. The pyramids were old before Rome was founded, before Homer wrote the Iliad, before the first Chinese dynasty. They have watched every empire rise, every civilization stumble, and every generation convince itself that it is the most important one. The pyramids have no opinion.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:198:  { text: `The Panama Canal required the removal of more earth than any construction project in history — over two hundred million cubic meters of rock and soil. The French tried first, starting in eighteen eighty-one. They failed catastrophically. Over twenty-two thousand workers died, mostly from malaria and yellow fever. The project bankrupted the French company and triggered a political scandal that nearly toppled the government. The Americans succeeded two decades later, largely because Colonel William Gorgas figured out that the diseases were carried by mosquitoes and drained the swamps. The canal is not just an engineering triumph. It is a monument to the twenty-two thousand people whose deaths taught the survivors that the real enemy was never the mountain — it was the puddles.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:199:  { text: `The Hoover Dam contains enough concrete to build a two-lane highway from San Francisco to New York. It was poured in interlocking columns rather than a single mass because a solid block of that size would have taken one hundred twenty-five years to cool and would have cracked from internal heat. The concrete at its core is still curing to this day. Twenty-one thousand men worked on it during the Great Depression, and ninety-six died during construction — though the official number was kept lower. The dam's generators provide electricity to one point three million people. It holds back nine point two trillion gallons of water. If it ever failed, the flood would reach Mexico.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:230:  { text: `During the great blizzard of nineteen seventy-seven in Buffalo, a retired nurse named Agnes walked two miles through chest-high snow to check on her neighbor, an elderly man who lived alone. She found him sitting in a freezing house — his furnace had failed. She wrapped him in every blanket she could find, boiled water on the stove for warmth, and stayed with him for three days until the roads were cleared. He survived. When asked why she'd risked her life, she said, “He shoveled my driveway every winter for fifteen years and never once let me thank him. I figured I owed him one.”`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['cinematic', 'minimal'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:265:  { text: `Niccolò Machiavelli wrote "The Prince" in fifteen thirteen as a practical guide to political power. Its most famous advice — "it is better to be feared than loved, if you cannot be both" — has been quoted by dictators and executives for five centuries. But Machiavelli was not a tyrant. He was a failed diplomat who had been imprisoned and tortured by the Medici family, and he wrote the book as a job application — a gift to the very people who had destroyed his career, hoping they would hire him back. The most influential treatise on power was written by a man who had none.`, type: 'History', styles: ['politics_power', 'dark_academia'] },
